WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.557
- Professor Steve Girvin, for those of you

00:00:02.557 --> 00:00:05.820
who don't know him, just
a legendary professor

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.470
here at Yale and someone who's
been a great partner to us

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.964
in terms of YQI and its connections

00:00:12.964 --> 00:00:15.240
to the Schmidt Program at Jackson.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.530
He's Yale's Eugene Higgins

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.170
Professor of Physics and Applied Physics.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.140
He's previously served as
Deputy Provost to the University

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.490
for Science and Technology,
Deputy Provost for Research,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.940
and his scholarship has
focused on theoretical studies

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.687
of quantum, many particle systems,

00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:34.560
interests in atomic physics,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.096
quantum optics, quantum computation,

00:00:37.096 --> 00:00:39.090
and in addition to teaching at Yale,

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.670
he has served as the founding Director

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.570
of Brookhaven National
Laboratories Co-Design Center

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.110
for Quantum Advantage, where
he remains an active member,

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.320
and this is a major national effort

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.060
funded by the Department of Energy

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.850
to accelerate transformational advances

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.390
in quantum science and to
build the tools necessary

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.660
to create scalable
quantum computer systems,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.600
so in terms of what that
means for global affairs.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.040
And very delighted to
hand it over to Steve

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.490
to introduce other colleagues.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.450
I know we have some folks here

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.540
from Professor Shruti
Puri's class as well,

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.070
so welcome to all of you,

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.660
and I think this will be a fun session,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:19.770
so appreciate it, Steve.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.136
- Thank you very much.

00:01:21.136 --> 00:01:24.300
This is, I hope this will be fun.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.940
So, I have some help.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.344
Shraddha Singh is a graduate student

00:01:30.344 --> 00:01:32.370
with me and Shruti Puri.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:36.960
Kevin Smith and Dan Price post-docs,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.960
and we have some physicists
here from Shruti's class.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:47.760
And so the first thing is the,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.290
you know, the most important
sentence you can learn

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:56.370
as a scientist and to succeed
is I don't understand.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:00.450
So, you know, we talk
this stuff every day.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.420
We have like kind of
inside baseball lingo.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.620
If I accidentally use
some or quantum mechanics

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.100
is just too strange for you,
stick your hand up and say,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.800
I don't understand, and we
have time and I wanna, I,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.800
you know, I'd rather uncover
a little than cover a lot.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.100
And so I also promise we
won't talk about this stuff.

00:02:25.223 --> 00:02:27.690
(room laughs)

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.127
And please, you know,

00:02:30.127 --> 00:02:33.393
just don't be shy and please interrupt.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.103
So I thought I'd, the way
this is gonna work is,

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:45.090
or we hope it's gonna
work, is I'll give a,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:49.207
I have a slide deck and the
first part is just kinda,

00:02:49.207 --> 00:02:51.510
you know, what's quantum about

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.850
and what's going on these days,

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:58.850
and then we're gonna talk
about a specific protocol

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.900
for encrypting communications in a way

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.740
that can't be broken without
violating the laws of physics,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.130
so it's a physical-based encryption,

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.260
and we're gonna, you have some handouts,

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.420
we're gonna do some little exercises,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.720
so I'll say some stuff
that'll sound complicated

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.580
and you'll get a chance to practice it,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.760
it'll seem easy, then
we'll move on, I hope.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.100
That's the plan, okay?

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.690
So there's, you know, there's a sort of

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.007
second quantum revolution
going on right now,

00:03:35.007 --> 00:03:40.007
and I want to just give you a
little background about that.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.670
So first, I have a few
places where I say vocabulary

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.103
just to make sure I'm using words

00:03:48.103 --> 00:03:51.630
in a way that you understand,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.630
and so when we say classical mechanics,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.270
we mean the things that
Galileo and Newton did

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:08.270
in the late 1600s, and it's
an extremely successful theory

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.560
of mechanical motions of
the moon, of falling apples,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.840
footballs, planets, and
when I say quantum mechanics

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.350
or the quantum theory,
that's a more complete theory

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:30.350
invented in the 20th century
that explains all of this,

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.250
but this fails for very small objects,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.860
and quantum theory applies to tiny objects

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.200
like atoms and electrons

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.223
and individual particles
of light, photons, okay?

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.180
And so quantum theory is
the single most successful

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.790
theory in all of the physical sciences.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:01.710
A theory is, you know, you have
evolution debates, you know,

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:06.423
theory is actually the highest
adjective, not the lowest.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.790
It means that it's been
fully confirmed by experiment

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.193
with many, many digits of accuracy.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.860
So, quantum theory describes
behavior at atomic scales

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.960
and now larger scales,

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.310
even millimeter scales
in some of the qubits,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.860
the quantum bits that we build,

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.710
and it's an extremely
strange and mysterious

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:36.510
and counterintuitive picture of the world

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.330
in which you have to use language

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:44.330
like particles act like and
waves act like particles,

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.640
and they're very counterintuitive
effects that the,

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.340
you can prepare exactly
the same experiment over

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.810
and over again and get different
random results each time,

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.030
even though it's exactly the same.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.410
There's a certain built-in
ineluctable randomness

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.890
in the results, and
then there's this thing

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:09.060
that Einstein hated,

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:13.973
spooky action at a distance,
entanglement, for which the,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.660
was discovered a long time ago,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.702
but for which really solid
experiments have only been done

00:06:22.702 --> 00:06:26.970
in the last 40 years and are the subject

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.883
of this year's Nobel Prize in Physics.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:35.070
So Einstein, you know,
invented a lot of the ideas

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.980
of quantum mechanics, but he hated it,

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:41.490
and he couldn't deal, he
just couldn't stand the fact

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.820
that there should be
non-deterministic results,

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.060
there should be ineluctable randomness,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.060
and he said, "God does not play dice,"

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:55.680
but modern physicists
like to say that the irony

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.084
is that it was Einstein
who gave God the dice.

00:06:59.084 --> 00:07:02.760
He really gave us a lot of the ideas

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.460
that founded the quantum theory.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:07.590
So the first quantum revolution,

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:11.070
the sort of invention
of the quantum theory,

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:13.479
beginning more than 100 years ago,

00:07:13.479 --> 00:07:16.470
people were, physicists were just thinking

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:21.470
about very basic things
about reality and electrons

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:26.220
and atoms and light, and there were,

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.340
it's just curiosity-driven
fundamental research.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.093
I want to put a plug in that
for you future policy leaders.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.500
There were no conceivable applications

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.953
for these kind of things,

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.160
thinking about things that were invisible,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:45.963
tiny atoms and so forth,

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:52.070
and yet it led to the
technological revolution

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:53.700
of the 20th century.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.700
It led to the transistor, the laser,

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:57.870
the atomic clock,

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.603
which in turn led to the
global positioning system.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:06.060
And the inventors of quantum
mechanics did not foresee

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.800
these quantum devices, and even
the more practical engineers

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.620
who invented these quantum devices

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.580
did not foresee their
applications, which are,

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.633
you know, all around you
and yet invisible, right?

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:27.470
So, you know, here's a picture
of Charlie Townes in 1953

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.810
with this big, clunky physics experiment

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:35.010
that was one of the
first atomic clocks that,

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.410
it's hard to imagine that today, you know,

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:40.590
I hold in my hand a gadget that knows,

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:44.010
by listening to transmissions
from satellites,

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.380
where it is on the, what time it is

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:49.200
to about a nanosecond and
where it is on the Earth

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:50.940
to a few meters of precision.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.023
It's just incredible.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.297
There's the first transistor, 1947.

00:08:57.297 --> 00:08:59.490
It was about this big.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:04.490
You can see beautiful
blobs of solder and wires,

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:10.070
and it's incredibly ugly, and
those people who invented that

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.473
could not foresee this and the,

00:09:16.473 --> 00:09:21.473
you know, the central processing
unit in your computer,

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.140
and they certainly could not foresee

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:29.043
the number of transistors being
produced in the world today.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:32.680
Any idea how many transistors are made

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:38.073
in one second every day of every year?

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.233
More than 20 trillion
transistors per second.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:46.920
It's a good thing they're small.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:47.820
(room laughs)

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.593
Otherwise we'd run out of stuff.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:55.790
So, you know, the scaling
from one at a centimeter scale

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:01.833
to 20, you know, there's
about roughly 20 billion

00:10:01.833 --> 00:10:03.933
in an iPhone.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:08.640
Depending on how you count, even more.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.510
It's just amazing and
very hard to foresee,

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:16.230
so we hope that this same
thing is going to happen

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:17.970
with the second quantum revolution,

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.850
but we have a long, long way to go.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:25.620
So, we've recently come to understand

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:29.340
that these quantum devices
aren't very quantum.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.830
They don't have the full power

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:35.088
that's available to quantum machines,

00:10:35.088 --> 00:10:39.600
and in particular, there's
much more power for processing

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.713
and communicating
information than we realized,

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.870
and so the new idea which is driving

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:50.880
this second quantum revolution

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.840
is that the very strangest
aspects of quantum theory,

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:56.670
this uncertainty and randomness,

00:10:56.670 --> 00:11:01.670
which I'll show you some
examples, they're not bugs.

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.660
Like, it seems like, to
a classical physicist,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.570
oh, you do an experiment
exactly the same way every time

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:11.040
and you get a random result?

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:11.940
That seems bad.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:15.300
I can't predict as well as I could before.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.360
You can predict the probabilities
of different results,

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:21.330
but you can't predict the
results of any experiment.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:22.350
It seems like a bug,

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:24.630
but in fact it can be a feature

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.900
that we can use in
technologies, and in fact,

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.790
that randomness is going
to be key to our quantum

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:35.700
encryption protocol that
we're gonna work through today

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:38.613
for securely transmitting information.

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:46.260
So this is a, I think
the final slide on this.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.260
So there's this revolution underway.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:50.580
I tried to explain,

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:53.160
we couldn't predict the
consequences of the first one,

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.590
so I don't think we can predict

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.660
the consequences of this one.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:01.530
It has the potential to fizzle
out because the technology

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:05.040
is just too difficult at this
point, or it has the potential

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:09.390
to, you know, perhaps be as revolutionary

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:13.050
as the tech revolution
of the coming century.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.295
And it involves sort of three things:

00:12:15.295 --> 00:12:20.295
quantum materials with novel
properties, quantum sensing.

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:25.230
Measurement is a very important
part of quantum mechanics.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:28.170
The role of an observer is special.

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:31.200
When you observe something, you change it.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:34.557
That's gonna be part of our
encryption protocol also.

00:12:34.557 --> 00:12:37.590
And we've learned much more about how to,

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:40.830
the ultimate limits of making
precision measurements,

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:44.610
and those are being used
both for fundamental physics,

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.520
things like seeing black hole mergers

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:50.884
producing tiny vibrations of spacetime,

00:12:50.884 --> 00:12:54.120
which we can now measure,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.190
and it's also the start, you know,

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.934
can be used in intelligence gathering.

00:12:58.934 --> 00:13:03.934
You can use a thing which is
kind of like an atomic clock

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.600
to fly over a mountain

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.400
and see whether there's a tunnel inside

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:11.040
because there's less
gravity than you expect

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:12.753
because there's a tunnel there.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:18.710
And then information
processing is very important,

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.380
and what's, a lot of what's
done in this institute

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.850
is centered around
quantum, building computers

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.280
based on the principles
of quantum mechanics,

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:31.290
but our discussion today is gonna be

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:34.743
about communication security and encrypt.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:41.070
Okay, so the technology is not here yet.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:44.340
We're in the very earliest days.

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:47.760
I wouldn't say we're in the
earliest part of the hype cycle,

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:51.030
but we're probably headed

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:54.030
for some troughs of disillusionment

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:58.383
before we finally get
this technology to work.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:03.880
But lots and lots of
money is being invested

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:10.070
in these technologies now
because of their great potential.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:16.500
Okay, so I'm gonna pause here.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:20.850
We're gonna start our discussion
of the main topic today,

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:24.870
secure communication, but
maybe if you have questions now

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:27.470
about what I've said so
far, I'm happy to take them.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:30.840
Yeah.

