00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:11.613
of offense and defense in signals.

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:14.866
Okay, so here's the transistor.

00:23:14.866 --> 00:23:17.305
Rather than a transistor I have just a,

00:23:17.305 --> 00:23:21.600
some mechanical switch, and you can store

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:24.480
one bit of information in
the position of the switch.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:27.985
If the switch is off, so the
light bulb is not shining,

00:23:27.985 --> 00:23:29.610
we'll call that zero.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:31.830
If the switch is closed so that circuit,

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:33.810
the current can flow from the battery

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:36.240
through the light bulb,
we'll call that a one,

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:39.180
and you can transmit
that bit to other people

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:43.350
in the form of the light
coming from the light bulb,

00:23:43.350 --> 00:23:46.345
so information is
physical, we like to say.

00:23:46.345 --> 00:23:49.083
It's always transmitted,

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:54.090
stored in and transmitted
by physical systems.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:56.490
So these, instead of
these mechanical switches,

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.986
you have electrical
switches that are controlled

00:23:59.986 --> 00:24:04.080
by electricity in the form of transistors

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:05.733
in your computers.

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:12.500
Okay, so there's one provable,
provably secure method

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:21.873
in classical communication,
meaning with ordinary bits,

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:26.820
to do secure information transmission,

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:31.820
and that's the one-time pad
encryption using a random key.

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:36.750
So Alice and Bob have a
pad of paper and it had,

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:41.263
they flipped coins or something
and they have written down

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:47.660
a completely random string
of bits, zeros and ones.

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:52.440
They've also encoded
their English language,

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:56.370
plain text message as a
string of zeros and ones

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:58.203
that's called the plain text, there,

00:25:00.025 --> 00:25:05.025
and then that text is encrypted
by the following rule.

00:25:08.976 --> 00:25:12.870
If the key is zero, take the plain text

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:14.970
and just copy it down here.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:18.720
If the key is one, copy the
opposite of what is here.

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:21.000
If it's a one, you put a zero.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:24.570
If it's a zero, you put a one, okay?

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:27.660
So it's as if there are two ways to,

00:25:27.660 --> 00:25:30.270
let's think of these as,
I don't know, yes and no.

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:33.000
There are two ways to encode yes and no.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:37.620
Zero is yes, no is one
or the other way around,

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:41.340
and for each bit, Alice is flipping a coin

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:44.103
to decide which code to use.

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:49.800
So once you've done that, this
string is completely random.

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:54.800
There is no way that you can,
even if you intercept it,

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:59.520
no way to know what it means
unless you have this key.

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:02.460
So if Bob has, if Alice has this key

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:04.860
and Bob has a copy of it,

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:08.790
Bob can go in the other direction
and decrypt very easily.

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:12.900
He looks at the key and
applies the same rule.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:15.630
If it's a zero, just copy it back up here.

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:18.570
If it's a one, copy the opposite.

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:21.750
So the two rules just
can, applying it twice

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:24.720
just cancels the effect of the encryption.

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:25.770
Does that make sense?

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:32.130
So it's provably, you know,
the weak link in all of this

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:35.100
is always the people, but mathematically,

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:36.870
if the people are behaving themselves,

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:40.050
mathematically, you can prove
this is perfectly secure

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:43.197
as long as the key is completely random.

00:26:43.197 --> 00:26:46.289
There's no pattern to the zeros and ones.

00:26:46.289 --> 00:26:49.230
It has to be as long as the message

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:52.263
because you have to
encrypt each single bit,

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:58.670
and you can never reuse it
because there'll be correlations

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:01.383
between subsequent messages if you do,

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:07.260
and so that's why it's
called a one-time pad, okay?

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:11.793
So that's basic classical encryption.

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:17.480
This is provably secure,
but it has a huge problem:

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:20.340
the key distribution problem.

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:25.170
The secret key, if Bob doesn't have it,

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:28.890
Alice has to send it to
Bob and it's a bit string

00:27:28.890 --> 00:27:31.400
that's the same length as the message.

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.360
If she can't send the message securely,

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:35.733
how could she send the
secret key securely?

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:40.242
So maybe Bob, you know,

00:27:40.242 --> 00:27:43.547
went back to somewhere in Virginia

00:27:43.547 --> 00:27:46.530
and copied down what she had on,

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:50.130
Alice had on her one-time
pad and kept it with him,

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:53.040
hopefully for safekeeping
and nobody saw it,

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:57.390
then it'll work, but if,

00:27:57.390 --> 00:28:00.480
if Alice and Bob are in
different corners of the globe

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.311
and haven't been able to do that,

00:28:03.311 --> 00:28:07.050
you're stuck, because you can't
distribute the secret key.

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:10.110
It's the same problem as secretly
distributing the message.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:11.160
Does that make sense?

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:17.750
Okay, so Eve could intercept
the telephone call,

00:28:19.268 --> 00:28:24.268
the internet transmission,
the optical fiber transmission

00:28:25.727 --> 00:28:30.727
and see what either the
message or the secret key are

00:28:31.605 --> 00:28:35.880
and then just send it on
or observe it as it goes by

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:38.400
or send it on to Bob,

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:42.030
and Bob is none the wiser that
the eavesdropper is there.

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:44.064
It has compromised the channel.

00:28:44.064 --> 00:28:46.714
So it turns out there's a solution

00:28:46.714 --> 00:28:50.010
to the key distribution problem

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:55.010
if you use a quantum
version of bits, okay,

00:28:55.672 --> 00:29:00.270
and this is a protocol that
was invented by my friend

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:04.380
Charlie Bennett and colleague
Gilles Brassard in 1984.

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:07.500
It was based on an older
idea called quantum money

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:08.970
invented by Steve Wiesner,

00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:13.563
who was the son of the Wiesner
who was the MIT President.

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:19.880
Didn't, couldn't get it
published until much later

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:24.060
after these guys had done their protocol.

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:26.610
Okay, so we're gonna go through this.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:28.140
There's lots to unpack here.

