You gotta find out what is a quantum bit,

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:32.790
et cetera, et cetera, but here's,

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:34.833
I just want to do the big picture.

00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:40.910
So, here's Alice in the Quantum
Institute, and this is Bob.

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:43.590
Bob is up the street in Jackson School.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:45.540
Eve is somewhere in between,

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:48.600
and there are two ways we can communicate.

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:53.370
There's a public channel,
meaning it's a telephone

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:57.990
or internet connection,
something that's potentially,

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:00.683
whose security is not guaranteed, okay?

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:04.530
And then there's a quantum channel,

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:07.653
which I'm gonna have to
explain to you what it is,

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:13.620
and Eve has access
potentially to both channels,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:17.250
and both channels are gonna
be part of the protocol.

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:18.210
So there's a part

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:22.500
that you don't need
quantum things to send.

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:24.610
You can just talk over the telephone

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:30.150
and anybody can listen in
and it can still be secure.

00:30:30.150 --> 00:30:31.830
This is an interesting aspect of it,

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:35.310
because it has this quantum channel, okay?

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:39.210
And we're gonna try to be
consistent in our color coding.

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:43.320
So green will mean
public channel, anybody,

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:44.910
it means classical,

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:48.300
and anybody could in
principle be listening in,

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:51.180
and then the red will mean quantum channel

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:54.573
and blue will mean
classical or not quantum.

00:30:56.410 --> 00:31:00.990
Okay, so here's the basic
idea I want you to hold onto

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:05.157
as we march through the complicated parts.

00:31:05.157 --> 00:31:09.180
In quantum mechanics, if you observe,

00:31:09.180 --> 00:31:14.180
that is measure the state of
a quantum system, it changes,

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:22.140
and it's the act of looking at something,

00:31:22.140 --> 00:31:25.320
like shining light on it
and bouncing it to your eye,

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:27.570
the something is so tiny and fragile

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:30.330
that shining a little
light on it changes it.

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:32.430
That's roughly the idea.

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:37.430
And this BB84 protocol,
it's set up to detect

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:44.250
the presence of the
eavesdropper who is observing

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:46.860
what's being sent on the quantum channel.

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:48.510
There are gonna be quantum bits,

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:52.200
which I have to explain to
you, that are sent along here,

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:55.890
and if Eve looks at them,
makes a measurement of them

00:31:55.890 --> 00:32:00.843
to see what secret key
is being sent to Bob,

00:32:02.218 --> 00:32:07.218
they will unwittingly modify
the state of those bits

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:13.920
and corrupt what, the information
that's being sent to Bob,

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:17.070
but there's part of the
protocol is that Bob and Alice

00:32:17.070 --> 00:32:21.207
will be able to detect
that that happened, okay,

00:32:21.207 --> 00:32:24.000
and then call it off if
there's somebody there.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:27.450
Okay, so again, information
is still physical,

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:29.970
even if it's quantum information.

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:32.863
Instead of storing
information in a, you know,

00:32:32.863 --> 00:32:35.880
a big written zero or
one on a piece of paper

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:38.490
or a switch being open or closed,

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:42.690
we're gonna store information
in maybe the space

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:47.690
of a single atom or a single
particle of light, a photon.

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:52.620
And quantum mechanics teaches
us that the energy levels

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:56.580
of an atom are discreet,
they're quantized, they're not,

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:59.100
they don't vary continuously,

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:01.500
and you can label them then with integers,

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:05.280
and the lowest two you can
call state zero and one,

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:08.100
so if the energy is the lowest possible,

00:33:08.100 --> 00:33:11.520
maybe that represents bit value zero,

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:16.520
and the first excited state
represents bit value one,

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:20.430
and there are many physical
systems that can hold this:

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:23.220
atoms, molecules, ions, photons.

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:26.820
Here at Yale, we work a lot
with electrical circuits,

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:28.380
superconducting electrical circuits.

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:33.130
Even small mechanical oscillators
can have quantized energy.

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:36.660
Okay, well, quantum bits are very, very,

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:38.370
very strange and mysterious,

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:41.700
so we have to, you know,
fasten your seat belts

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:45.030
and we have to think about
these very non-intuitive things.

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:48.660
So in addition to having
the possibility of being

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:53.660
in state zero or state one,
there's an infinite number,

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:59.810
a continuous range of states
called superposition states,

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:05.590
which are in some sense
intermediate between zero and one,

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:11.640
and yet if you measure
which state it's in,

00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:14.460
it always comes out to be zero or one.

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:17.100
So, for the purposes of this protocol,

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:21.720
we're gonna use only four
states and we're gonna,

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:24.420
they're called polarization states

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:27.310
and we're gonna represent
them by arrows, so.

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:32.220
The qubit, the atom or
whatever is holding the qubit,

00:34:32.220 --> 00:34:34.800
the arrow can point up or down

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:36.720
or to the right or to the left.

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:41.160
Those are the only four states
that we're gonna talk about.

00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:44.220
If you look online at
videos about this protocol,

00:34:44.220 --> 00:34:47.640
they used to talk about
photon polarizations,

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:52.322
and they used these lines
with arrows on both ends

00:34:52.322 --> 00:34:56.400
and slopes like this, those four symbols,

00:34:56.400 --> 00:34:57.630
but it just doesn't matter.

00:34:57.630 --> 00:35:00.303
There's four symbols, okay?

00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:06.260
And there's one bit of
information in here,

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:09.660
a zero or one, and yet
there are four states,

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:13.920
and it's because they
represent two different ways

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:15.630
to put the zero and one in there,

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:19.230
just like in the one-time pad,

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:23.190
you could start with a
a zero in the plain text

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:26.070
and represent it either as a zero or one

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:27.690
in the encrypted text,

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:30.930
randomly switching between the encodings.

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:33.570
There are two kinds of encoding.

00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:36.600
Just like the secret
key in the one-time pad

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:40.110
can be a zero or one, those
are two different encodings,

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:42.780
we're gonna talk about a Z basis in coding

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:45.240
or an X basis in coding,

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:50.240
and so if Alice wants
to send a zero to Bob,

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:58.173
she can choose the Z encoding
and send him an up arrow,

00:35:58.173 --> 00:36:00.810
or she could choose the X encoding

00:36:00.810 --> 00:36:05.040
and send him an arrow
pointing to the right.

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.050
She wants to send a one,

00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:09.480
and she randomly chooses this encoding.

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:11.820
She sends a down arrow.

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:13.860
She randomly chooses the X encoding.

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:16.073
She sends a left arrow, okay?

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:20.700
So the blue things are
the classical bits value

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:22.350
she wants to send to Bob,

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:26.340
and the red things are the
quantum states of our bits.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:30.570
All right, so I think
we have an exercise now.

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:33.870
The conversion table is at the top,

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:37.200
and the exercise on the left

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:42.200
is you want to take the
classical bit and encode it

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:47.390
in the Z encoding into
one of those four arrows

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:50.670
or a zero and put it in the X encoding

00:36:50.670 --> 00:36:52.350
with one of those four arrows,

00:36:52.350 --> 00:36:54.780
and on the right you wanna
do the reverse process.

00:36:54.780 --> 00:36:55.740
Here are the arrows.

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:57.630
You want to put zeros and ones here.

00:36:57.630 --> 00:36:59.790
Does that make sense?

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:02.970
So take your time, feel
free to ask questions.

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:06.240
We have experts in the audience

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:08.133
who are standing by to assist you.

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:12.120
I think the next slide is the answer.

00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:16.890
Okay, so you can check your answers there.

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:19.020
It's okay to be wrong and ask questions

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:20.010
about why you're wrong.

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:20.883
That's allowed.

00:37:23.250 --> 00:37:26.820
That's the other important
sentence for every scientist.

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:27.990
I tell my students,

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:29.880
first thing when you get
out of bed in the morning,

00:37:29.880 --> 00:37:32.580
say to yourself, could I be wrong?

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:37.580
The easiest person to fool is
yourself when doing science.

00:37:38.370 --> 00:37:43.370
So, in the one-time pad, Alice
had flipped a bunch of coins

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:49.067
and had the secret key
and used those to randomly

00:37:49.067 --> 00:37:53.580
change the encoding for, in each bit.

00:37:53.580 --> 00:37:55.710
Here, Alice is gonna
do something different.

00:37:55.710 --> 00:38:00.060
She's gonna randomly switch
between the Z encoding,

00:38:00.060 --> 00:38:03.270
where the bits are up and
down, and the X encoding,

00:38:03.270 --> 00:38:06.740
where they're left and right,
the polarizations, okay?

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:09.750
Now you say, well, that's not gonna help

00:38:09.750 --> 00:38:11.850
because Bob can just look at the arrow

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.330
and see whether it's
up, down, right or left.

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:17.490
Well, it doesn't work that way.

00:38:17.490 --> 00:38:19.290
The rules of quantum mechanics

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:24.290
don't let you know both of those things.

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:27.150
The rules of quantum mechanics say,

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:31.650
when Bob gets a qubit and
wants to see what its value is,

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:34.410
he has to make a decision.

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:38.520
He has to choose to see what the value is

00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:40.110
in the Z polarization

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:44.280
to make what we call
a Z measurement, or X,

00:38:44.280 --> 00:38:45.960
but he can't do both.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:48.180
So if you know where a particle is,

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:51.150
you can't know how fast
it's going or vice versa.

00:38:51.150 --> 00:38:52.710
This is like that.

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:56.640
If you know what the value of the bit is

00:38:56.640 --> 00:38:59.220
when you measure it and make a Z,

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:01.290
I'm gonna show you a
picture of a Z measurement.

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:06.233
When you ask what is the value
of the qubit in the Z basis,

00:39:07.140 --> 00:39:10.980
you remove the possibility
that you can find out

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:12.360
what it is in the X basis.

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:14.733
So it's like position and momentum.

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:19.743
There's a Heisenberg on
circuit principal connecting.

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:21.503
Yeah.

00:39:21.503 --> 00:39:22.560
- [Student] And it's the choice

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:24.780
for each and every bit, or is it?

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:25.680
- Each and every bit.

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:28.080
We're gonna, Bob has to measure

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:30.540
and he has to make a choice 'cause he,

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:34.920
and you'll see, you know,
I'm gonna go through it,

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:38.423
so keep asking questions, but the first,

00:39:39.807 --> 00:39:42.930
the thing you have to know
right now is he has a choice.

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:46.860
He can either measures in
the Z basis or the X basis.

00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:49.050
Now we're gonna see what does that imply?

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:51.360
What happens when he does that?

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:55.470
Okay, so here's, Bob has some setting

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:57.090
on his measurement apparatus.

00:39:57.090 --> 00:40:01.260
He can set it to Z or X, okay?

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:04.890
So he sets it to Z, and
it happens that Alice

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:07.980
has sent him a bunch
of bits in the Z basis,

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:11.250
so you see the arrows
are up and down, okay?

00:40:11.250 --> 00:40:12.540
What happens?

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:17.540
Well, they just go through
and they're presented to Bob

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:24.650
with, you know, this represents a zero

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:29.910
and there's another zero
and there's a one, okay?

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:34.380
So when Bob looks at the
state, it doesn't get messed up

00:40:34.380 --> 00:40:39.090
or changed, and Bob correctly
learns the bit value

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:41.133
that Alice sent, okay?

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:47.250
Something strange happens if
he chooses the wrong encoding.

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:50.550
If he decides to make an X measurement

00:40:50.550 --> 00:40:54.986
when the polarization
is in the Z direction,

00:40:54.986 --> 00:40:57.663
he gets random results.

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:01.800
Sometimes it's pointing to the right,

00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:06.780
sometimes it's pointing
to the left, and so,

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:11.070
but he doesn't know that he's
randomly changed the state.

00:41:11.070 --> 00:41:13.052
This would've been the result

00:41:13.052 --> 00:41:16.830
if this polarization had been to the right

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:18.960
and passed through the measurement

00:41:18.960 --> 00:41:20.670
and he got this unchanged.

00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:24.000
He doesn't know that he changed
it, there's no way to tell,

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:28.793
so the bottom line is he's
unsure what Alice sent, okay?

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:31.323
It's unpredictable.

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:37.230
And so there's this strange
thing in quantum mechanics:

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:40.740
the results are random, but
it's not like, you know,

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:45.450
I have an envelope, I
try to flip the coin,

00:41:45.450 --> 00:41:48.150
put a zero or one in a piece
of paper in the envelope

00:41:48.150 --> 00:41:49.950
and handed it to me and there's definitely

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:51.510
either a zero or one in the envelope,

00:41:51.510 --> 00:41:54.510
and when I open it, I see what it is.

00:41:54.510 --> 00:41:57.450
That is, when you open
it, you see what you get.

00:41:57.450 --> 00:42:01.590
In quantum mechanics, you
get what you see, you,

00:42:02.790 --> 00:42:06.604
it doesn't have a value,
neither zero or one,

00:42:06.604 --> 00:42:11.310
but when you look, the act
of looking brings a value

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:13.320
into existence: zero, one,

00:42:13.320 --> 00:42:17.520
and if you ask is it
pointing in the X direction,

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:20.759
is it pointing horizontally, it always is,

00:42:20.759 --> 00:42:24.090
sometimes to the right,
sometimes to the left.

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:27.060
If you ask, is it pointing up or down,

00:42:27.060 --> 00:42:29.490
it always is when you measure it.

00:42:29.490 --> 00:42:32.850
All right, so it works the other way too.

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:36.780
If Bob chooses to make an X measurement

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:39.570
and he happens to receive bits

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:42.623
that are pointing left and
right, it works fine, okay?

00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:49.410
He correctly learns the classical
value bit that Alice sent.

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:52.350
But if he makes a Z measurement on qubits

00:42:52.350 --> 00:42:56.610
that were encoded in the X basis,

00:42:56.610 --> 00:43:01.263
he accidentally and unknowingly
randomly changes them, okay?

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:06.120
All right, so how can we use
these facts about nature,

00:43:06.120 --> 00:43:09.510
which I just have to ask
you to accept at face value

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:12.033
because they're completely insane.

00:43:13.732 --> 00:43:15.600
Alice is gonna, how can we use it?

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:17.490
So Alice is gonna randomly switch.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:22.020
She flips a coin and decides
whether to use the Z basis

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:25.874
or the X basis, and she
does not tell Bob in advance

00:43:25.874 --> 00:43:29.010
what she's gonna do, okay,

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:33.270
so there's no need for, you
know, communication of that.

00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:35.613
That would be the same
as the key, all right?

00:43:36.450 --> 00:43:41.250
So, Bob doesn't know if Alice

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:44.610
chose to encode in the
Z basis or the X basis,

00:43:44.610 --> 00:43:47.070
so he doesn't know which basis to use

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:50.190
in order to measure to
get the right answer,

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:53.760
so he just guesses, okay?

00:43:53.760 --> 00:43:56.220
He could guess a Z every
time or X every time,

00:43:56.220 --> 00:44:00.060
or he could flip a coin,
and about half the time,

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:03.300
he'll by chance guess correctly

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:05.993
and half the time incorrectly, okay?

00:44:08.107 --> 00:44:09.720
And so if he guesses correctly,

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:12.120
he gets the correct bit value.

00:44:12.120 --> 00:44:16.613
If he guesses incorrectly, he
gets a random bit value, okay?

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:21.510
So there's a, even though it's random,

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:23.065
it might actually be the right one.

00:44:23.065 --> 00:44:25.590
It might, you know, if it
was supposed to be zero,

00:44:25.590 --> 00:44:28.770
maybe it comes out zero, so there's a 50%.

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:32.010
But he guesses incorrectly
only 50% of the time,

00:44:32.010 --> 00:44:33.903
so that's where the 25% is.

