01:02:16.800 --> 01:02:19.980
- Yes, you were, and that
was an interesting case here

01:02:19.980 --> 01:02:22.980
because they compared the first bits

01:02:22.980 --> 01:02:24.420
and it was the same and they was like,

01:02:24.420 --> 01:02:27.734
oh, we're not spied on, and
if they had stopped here,

01:02:27.734 --> 01:02:29.370
Eve could have gotten the information.

01:02:29.370 --> 01:02:30.870
They were like no, let's try another one,

01:02:30.870 --> 01:02:32.610
and then the second bits were wrong,

01:02:32.610 --> 01:02:33.990
and so that's why they
knew they were spied on.

01:02:33.990 --> 01:02:36.660
Okay, group number four, Bob four?

01:02:36.660 --> 01:02:37.650
Spied on or not?

01:02:37.650 --> 01:02:39.240
- [Student] I decided not.

01:02:39.240 --> 01:02:40.110
- That's correct.

01:02:40.110 --> 01:02:42.090
Nobody listened to you.

01:02:42.090 --> 01:02:43.953
Group five, Bob five.

01:02:45.090 --> 01:02:46.440
- [Student] Yes, I was spied on.

01:02:46.440 --> 01:02:48.150
- Yes, you're correct.

01:02:48.150 --> 01:02:50.215
And I think we had
another group six, maybe?

01:02:50.215 --> 01:02:51.048
- [Student] Yeah.

01:02:51.048 --> 01:02:52.317
- Group six, were you spied on or not?

01:02:52.317 --> 01:02:53.334
- [Student] Yes.

01:02:53.334 --> 01:02:54.350
- No, you are not.

01:02:54.350 --> 01:02:56.141
(room cross-talks)

01:02:56.141 --> 01:02:56.974
Oh, okay.

01:02:56.974 --> 01:02:58.641
- [Student A] Their Michel
overheard incorrectly

01:02:58.641 --> 01:03:01.745
that Eve was a spy,
that Eve wasn't spying.

01:03:01.745 --> 01:03:04.245
(room laughs)

01:03:09.067 --> 01:03:10.400
- [Student B] Oops.

01:03:10.400 --> 01:03:12.210
- These are the groups,
there were six groups, right,

01:03:12.210 --> 01:03:13.043
I think, okay.

01:03:14.007 --> 01:03:16.064
But that's a good example on like,

01:03:16.064 --> 01:03:18.517
it's much easier to discard the whole key

01:03:18.517 --> 01:03:21.424
if you have a doubt than
just using it regardless.

01:03:21.424 --> 01:03:22.680
- Right, right.

01:03:22.680 --> 01:03:23.880
Okay, super.

01:03:23.880 --> 01:03:24.840
Thanks, Florian.

01:03:24.840 --> 01:03:26.610
So here's the last slide.

01:03:26.610 --> 01:03:29.160
This is just to illustrate for you

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:32.703
this in a real experiment.

01:03:34.680 --> 01:03:39.680
So, China has this amazing
Micius satellite in orbit

01:03:40.800 --> 01:03:45.570
about four years ago, I think, 2018,

01:03:45.570 --> 01:03:49.647
and they have these telescopes

01:03:52.170 --> 01:03:57.170
which follow the satellite
and lasers which send photons,

01:03:57.780 --> 01:04:00.300
individual photons up at the satellite,

01:04:00.300 --> 01:04:02.720
which then sends them back down,

01:04:02.720 --> 01:04:07.720
in this case to Vienna to Anton
Zeilinger's lab in Vienna.

01:04:08.910 --> 01:04:12.240
Anton is, won the Nobel Prize this year

01:04:12.240 --> 01:04:17.240
for work on quantum entanglement,

01:04:17.700 --> 01:04:20.883
which is kind of the
background behind this.

01:04:22.140 --> 01:04:26.790
And they were able to send,
it's a moving satellite,

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:28.401
you know, it's a small target.

01:04:28.401 --> 01:04:29.903
You gotta send photons up

01:04:29.903 --> 01:04:32.190
and hit the detector there and so forth.

01:04:32.190 --> 01:04:34.200
It's pretty complicated.

01:04:34.200 --> 01:04:38.863
And they were able to send
a few thousand secret bits,

01:04:39.855 --> 01:04:44.855
which were then used
to encode a phone call

01:04:45.510 --> 01:04:48.060
between the President of,

01:04:48.060 --> 01:04:51.930
Anton was the President of the
Austrian Academy of Sciences,

01:04:51.930 --> 01:04:55.770
to the President of the
Chinese Academy of Sciences.

01:04:55.770 --> 01:04:58.680
And here's a little animation.

01:04:58.680 --> 01:04:59.960
Oh okay, there we go.

01:04:59.960 --> 01:05:02.370
So there's a satellite
coming up over the horizon.

01:05:02.370 --> 01:05:04.320
Here's the laser beam from the telescope,

01:05:04.320 --> 01:05:07.110
and you see the dome turning as it goes.

01:05:07.110 --> 01:05:08.730
This is a kind of time lapse thing.

01:05:08.730 --> 01:05:12.060
That's why it's stretching out like that.

01:05:12.060 --> 01:05:17.060
Very technologically complex
to send individual photons

01:05:18.330 --> 01:05:21.730
up to this thing that's
moving 18,000 miles an hour

01:05:23.010 --> 01:05:26.700
and then have it be able to send others

01:05:26.700 --> 01:05:31.700
down from space to Vienna.

01:05:32.220 --> 01:05:36.330
So this is the current state
of the art in this technology.

01:05:36.330 --> 01:05:40.113
It's also been used in optical fiber.

01:05:42.090 --> 01:05:45.933
There's a company in Europe, ID Quantique,

01:05:45.933 --> 01:05:48.030
that Nicolas Gisin founded

01:05:48.030 --> 01:05:52.140
that has done tests between
banks in Switzerland

01:05:52.140 --> 01:05:55.290
sending encrypted bits
through optical fiber

01:05:55.290 --> 01:05:57.360
over tens of kilometers.

01:05:57.360 --> 01:05:58.650
This is the light.

01:05:58.650 --> 01:06:01.650
The advantage of using the satellite

01:06:01.650 --> 01:06:04.020
is you only have to go
through a thin layer

01:06:04.020 --> 01:06:04.983
of the atmosphere, right?

01:06:04.983 --> 01:06:06.690
The atmosphere is very, very thin.

01:06:06.690 --> 01:06:10.740
It's just a few miles of air,
and then you go through back,

01:06:10.740 --> 01:06:13.050
but the disadvantage is
you gotta hit that target,

01:06:13.050 --> 01:06:15.030
and most of the photons will miss,

01:06:15.030 --> 01:06:17.940
so they have to do this many times.

01:06:17.940 --> 01:06:20.010
And then optical fiber is amazing.

01:06:20.010 --> 01:06:23.310
You can send light through
it for several kilometers

01:06:23.310 --> 01:06:25.170
without much of it being absorbed,

01:06:25.170 --> 01:06:28.143
but if you want to send
it across the Pacific,

01:06:29.550 --> 01:06:32.940
you have to have amplifiers
that can keep it going,

01:06:32.940 --> 01:06:35.640
and those amplifiers ruin the classic,

01:06:35.640 --> 01:06:38.520
the quantum part and make
it classical, so it's,

01:06:38.520 --> 01:06:40.980
you have to build something
called a quantum repeater,

01:06:40.980 --> 01:06:44.010
and we haven't, we're
learning how to do that now.

01:06:44.010 --> 01:06:45.480
It's very, very hard.

01:06:45.480 --> 01:06:48.480
So it's still very early
days for the technology,

01:06:48.480 --> 01:06:52.680
but these, this particular
encoding, you know,

01:06:52.680 --> 01:06:55.770
is in actual use and actually works.

01:06:55.770 --> 01:06:58.170
So I hope you had fun
and learned a few things.

01:06:58.170 --> 01:06:59.003
Thank you.

01:06:59.003 --> 01:07:01.570
(room applauds)

